  "query_preview": "../Monday through Thursday/...\nAn active fall-time weather pattern will continue across \nSEAK next week. Monday will start off with a ridge of high\npressure over most of the panhandle, which could lead to fog\ndevelopment, with weaker onshore flow and light showers ahead of \nthe next system. A progressive low and cold front will trek \nacross the Gulf and reach Yakutat Monday afternoon before \nsweeping southeast across the panhandle Monday night into early\nTuesday before weakening and stalling across southern panhandle,\nwith onshore flow behind bringing steady rain into the panhandle.\nTotal precipitation with this initial system will remain \nrelatively limited, though a brief period of moderate to heavy \nrain and increasing southerly winds will develop ahead of the \nfrontal boundary. Behind this initial system, a stronger low is \nforecast to deepen rapidly (~960 mb) as it lifts northeast into \nthe northern Gulf of Alaska on Tuesday, stalling through midweek \nbefore gradually weakening and dissipating on Thursday. This \nstrong low will quickly send another front and deeper moisture \nfeed toward and through SEAK Tuesday into Wednesday, bringing \nmoderate to heavy rain and strong gusty winds, along with severe \nmarine hazards. South to southeasterly winds will intensify \nsignificantly on Tuesday, producing gale-force conditions in inner\nchannels such as northern Lynn Canal and Clarence Strait, with \ngales (34-47 kt) and possibly storm-force winds (48\u201355 kts) and \ncombined seas exceeding 20 feet along outer coastal waters, mainly\nfrom Cape Spencer northward. By Thursday, conditions will improve\nbehind the frontal passage and as the low in the northern Gulf \nweakens, though lingering onshore flow will sustain rain showers, \nwhile winds and seas slowly subside.\n\n\nPersistent onshore flow with mainly cloudy skies and rain will \nkeep temperatures cool and consistent through the period, with \ndaytime highs in the mid to upper 40s (low 50s possible on\nTuesday) and overnight lows in the upper 30s to low 40s.",
